<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Golconda Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1518 CE – 1687 CE · capital Golconda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Qutb Shahi sultanate of the eastern Deccan, founder of Hyderabad and the Charminar, enriched by the world-famous Golconda diamonds.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quli-qutb-mulk">Sultan Quli</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> noble from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>. As Bahmani authority collapsed he ruled independently from the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> from 1518, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty">Qutb Shahi dynasty</a>, which formally adopted the royal title under his successors.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty">Qutb Shahs</a> were Shia Muslims who governed a largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-language">Telugu</a>-speaking population and patronised Telugu as well as Persian and Dakhini literature. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-quli-qutb-shah">Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> in 1591 and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charminar">Charminar</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> was famous for its diamond mines, which produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koh-i-noor">Koh-i-Noor</a>, and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textiles exported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machilipatnam">Masulipatnam</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty">Qutb Shahs</a> into tribute in 1636. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> for eight months and took it by treachery in 1687, imprisoning the last sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-hasan-qutb-shah">Abul Hasan Tana Shah</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> later became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizams</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/golconda-sultanate.jpg" alt="Golconda Sultanate" width="320" height="183"><figcaption>Image: LG02 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qutbshahi_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1518 CE – 1687 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Golconda</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sultan Quli, Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah, Abul Hasan Tana Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Telugu, Dakhini</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qutb Shahi dynasty, Qutb Shahis, Sultanate of Golconda</dd></dl></aside></div>
